# Firebase & Data Layer Rules

## Where things live
- Firestore/Stripe/Auth calls exist ONLY inside `data/` classes that implement
  a `domain/` repository interface. Never call `FirebaseFirestore.instance`
  or `FirebaseAuth.instance` from a Bloc, use case, or widget.
- Stripe secret keys and payment-capture logic live only in Cloud Functions.
  The client only ever calls a callable function — never a Stripe secret
  endpoint directly.

## Firestore access patterns
- Every write from the client must be shaped so a Security Rule can validate
  it (e.g., `customerId` is always `request.auth.uid`, never client-chosen
  for another user).
- Prefer transactions (`runTransaction`) for any read-then-write that must be
  atomic (e.g., accepting an order — see `acceptOrderTxn`).
- Admin-only reads are gated by Firebase Auth custom claims
  (`request.auth.token.admin == true`) in Security Rules — never by a
  client-readable `role` field alone.

## Mapping
- Every Firestore document has an explicit DTO class + `fromFirestore` /
  `toFirestore` mapping. Never pass raw `Map<String, dynamic>` above the
  data layer.
- Timestamps are converted to `DateTime` at the mapping boundary — domain
  and presentation never see `Timestamp`.

## Error handling
- Wrap every Firebase call in try/catch; map `FirebaseException` /
  `FirebaseAuthException` to the sealed `Failure` type from
  `001-architecture-clean-code.mdc`. Never let a raw platform exception
  escape the data layer.

## Security Rules file
- Every new collection or field access pattern added to the app must have a
  corresponding rule added to `firestore.rules` in the same PR — the rules
  file is source of truth, not an afterthought.
- No rule should read `allow read, write: if true;` even temporarily —
  use the Firebase Emulator for local testing instead of loosening prod rules.
